 unread (from 278 down to 261). Total 150.\nPage 4 (token 261): 50 unread. Total 200. Running unread 68.\nPage 5 (token 211): 50 unread. Total 250. Running 118.\nPage 6 (token 161): 50 unread. Total 300. Running 168.\nPage 7 (token 111): 50 unread. Total 350. Running 218.\nPage 8 (token 61): 50 unread. Total 400. Running 268.\nPage 9 (token 11): 10 unread. Total 410. Running 278.\n\nSo inbox has 410 emails, 278 unread.\n\nLet me double check page 3 count of unread: entries from index 278 down to 261 inclusive = 278-261+1 = 18. Yes.\n\nAnd page 3''s first 32 entries were read (Kestrel etc. through 279). 50 - 18 = 32 read. Yes.\n\nSo: 278 unread out of 410 in inbox.\n\nImportant nuance: essentially all 278 unread are \"Edge Sender\" test fixtures from 1–12 June 2024 — synthetic edge-case messages (MIME torture tests, 200-message \"Long thread\", attachment bombs, etc.). Real mail is all read. That''s the key insight: the unread count is inflated by fixtures, not by neglected correspondence.\n\nLet me verify: are there any unread non-Edge emails? Page 3 unread: all Edge Sender. Pages 4-8: all Edge Sender. Page 9: Edge Sender, \"First\", \"lf@edge.test\", \"garbage@edge.test\", \"b@edge.test\", \"a@edge.test\", empty from. All fixtures. So yes — zero unread real mail.\n\nAlso note the \"Long thread\" thread has 200 messages (edge-thread-200) — that''s a big chunk of the count. Let me estimate: the Long thread spans from 09:05 (id 51) to 12:24 (id 250) = 200 messages. Yes, exactly 200. So 200 of the 278 unread are one synthetic thread.\n\nOther unread: 18 (page 3, ids 261-278) + 10 (page 9) + ... let''s compute: 278 total - 200 long thread = 78 others. Page 3''s 18 + page 9''s 10 = 28, plus pages 4-8 contain the long thread plus others. Let''s not over-detail; I can say ~200 of them are a single synthetic \"Long thread\" fixture.\n\nActually let me verify the long thread count: ids 51 through 250 inclusive = 200. And they''re all \"Re: Long thread\" except id 51 which is \"Long thread\". Yes, 200 messages.\n\nSo: 278 unread = 200 (one Long thread fixture) + 78 other fixtures.\n\nAlso worth noting: the inbox total 410, and the oldest are dated 1970/9999 (date-epoch, date-year-9999) — sorting quirks.\n\nAnswer: 278 unread, all fixtures, zero real unread mail. Offer to mark them read / archive.\n\nFormat: lead with the number."}],"createdAt":1794332188808},{"type":"text","text":"**278 unread** in `ana@demo.trymetis.email` — out of **410 emails** in the inbox. But the number is misleading: **not one of them is real mail.**\n\nI paged the whole inbox (9 pages, 50 at a time) and checked every message''s read state. The split is stark:\n\n| | Count | Read state |\n|---|---|---|\n| Real correspondence (Kestrel, Konkan Bank, Raj Mehta, Priya Nair, Brightloom, Codeyard, Aozora…) | 132 | **all read** |\n| Synthetic test fixtures from `Edge Sender` / `edge.test` | 278 | **all unread** |\n\nSo your actual unread count is **zero**. Everything unread arrived on **1–12 June 2024** from a test harness, and it''s all deliberately malformed mail:\n\n- **200 of the 278 are a single thread** — `edge-thread-200`, subject \"Long thread\", one message per minute from 09:05 to 12:24 on 10 Jun 2024. That one fixture alone is 72% of your unread badge.\n- The other 78 are MIME and encoding torture tests: `mime-nested-100`, `enc-invalid-utf8`, `att-pkpass-zip-bomb`, `att-pdf-2000-pages`, `max-attachments-500`, `max-recipients-4000`, `img-dimensions-10000x10000`, plus a 100k-character folded subject and a 998-character run of \"S\".\n- A few have deliberately broken metadata: one dated **1 Jan 1970** (`date-epoch`), one dated **31 Dec 9999** (`date-year-9999`), one with no sender, one with no subject, one with garbage headers. Those are why the inbox doesn''t sort cleanly at the ends.\n\nTwo practical consequences: any \"unread\" filter you build will return nothing but noise, and a workflow triggered on unread mail would fire 278 times on fixtures. If you want the badge gone, I can mark all 278 read in one call — or archive them, which is cleaner if you''d rather they leave the inbox entirely. Say which and I''ll do it.","createdAt":1794332188808}],"metadata":{"modelId":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash:floor","provider":"openai-compatible.chat"},"content":"**278 unread** in `ana@demo.trymetis.email` — out of **410 emails** in the inbox. But the number is misleading: **not one of them is real mail.**\n\nI paged the whole inbox (9 pages, 50 at a time) and checked every message''s read state. The split is stark:\n\n| | Count | Read state |\n|---|---|---|\n| Real correspondence (Kestrel, Konkan Bank, Raj Mehta, Priya Nair, Brightloom, Codeyard, Aozora…) | 132 | **all read** |\n| Synthetic test fixtures from `Edge Sender` / `edge.test` | 278 | **all unread** |\n\nSo your actual unread count is **zero**. Everything unread arrived on **1–12 June 2024** from a test harness, and it''s all deliberately malformed mail:\n\n- **200 of the 278 are a single thread** — `edge-thread-200`, subject \"Long thread\", one message per minute from 09:05 to 12:24 on 10 Jun 2024. That one fixture alone is 72% of your unread badge.\n- The other 78 are MIME and encoding torture tests: `mime-nested-100`, `enc-invalid-utf8`, `att-pkpass-zip-bomb`, `att-pdf-2000-pages`, `max-attachments-500`, `max-recipients-4000`, `img-dimensions-10000x10000`, plus a 100k-character folded subject and a 998-character run of \"S\".\n- A few have deliberately broken metadata: one dated **1 Jan 1970** (`date-epoch`), one dated **31 Dec 9999** (`date-year-9999`), one with no sender, one with no subject, one with garbage headers. Those are why the inbox doesn''t sort cleanly at the ends.\n\nTwo practical consequences: any \"unread\" filter you build will return nothing but noise, and a workflow triggered on unread mail would fire 278 times on fixtures. If you want the badge gone, I can mark all 278 read in one call — or archive them, which is cleaner if you''d rather they leave the inbox entirely. Say which and I''ll do it."}','assistant','v2','2026-11-10T17:35:04.482Z','f0c4be98-b2fd-59d7-b11e-c921e7d22615');
