hese are the same store mounted six ways, fine; if they''re six independent copies, edits won''t propagate and you''ll get divergence. E.g., edit Budget 2025.xlsx on the FTP drive and the S3 copies keep the old numbers. That''s the practical implication.\n\nAlso drive 3''s scoping: it only sees Clients/, so anything you put in Team/ or Finance/ won''t be visible there.\n\nLet me write the answer with a comparison table.\n\nTable: rows = drives, columns = root items, Team items, Finance/2025, Clients, edge/, timestamps.\n\nLet me be precise about counts:\n- D1 SFTP 36600459: root 4 (Clients, Finance, Team, welcome.txt); Team 6; Finance/2025 3; Clients 3; Aozora 2; no edge.\n- D2 FTP d4dacf11: same, no edge.\n- D3 FTP 766b212a (scoped): root 1 (Clients); Clients 3; Aozora 2; nothing else visible.\n- D4 FTP 42ffd478: same as D1 pattern, no edge.\n- D5 WebDAV cc3fbded: same, no edge.\n- D6 S3 b4199497: root 5 (+edge); Team 6; Finance/2025 3; Clients 3; Aozora 2; edge 15.\n- D7 S3 1ae0b7b1: same as D6.\n\nTotal file inventory per full mirror: welcome.txt (1) + Team files (4) + Team folders (2) + Finance/2025 files (3) + Clients subfolders (3) + Aozora (1 file + 1 folder) + Kaltwasser + Saffron & Sage (unopened) + edge (15 at top).\n\nI''ll present a clean answer.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have depth-3 evidence. Summary:\n\nSix drives (1 SFTP, 2 FTP, 4 FTP, 5 WebDAV, 6 S3, 7 S3) carry an identical tree with identical names and identical byte sizes:\n- welcome.txt (182)\n- Team/ → website/ (folder), Offsite 2026/ (folder), 新しいロゴ案.png (11491), voice-memo.wav (88244), brand-tokens.json (240), Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md (574)\n- Finance/ → 2025/ → Budget 2025.xlsx (1819), expenses.tsv (357), invoices.csv (440)\n- Clients/ → Aozora Ceramics/ → Proposal - signed.pdf (789), Rebrand/; Kaltwasser GmbH/; Saffron & Sage/\n\nDrives 6 & 7 (S3) additionally have edge/ (15 entries, identical in both).\n\nDrive 3 (FTP, folder-scoped) exposes only Clients/ — and Clients/Aozora Ceramics/ has exactly the same two entries (Proposal - signed.pdf 789 + Rebrand/). So drive 3 is a scoped subset of the same tree, not a different collection.\n\nDifferences are metadata-only:\n- mimeType: null on FTP and S3; accurate on SFTP (text/markdown, text/tab-separated-values); coarser on WebDAV (Meeting notes → text/plain, expenses.tsv → text/plain).\n- modifiedAt: null on all FTP drives; SFTP & WebDAV stamp everything 2026-11-10T17:28:49Z; both S3 buckets stamp 2026-11-10T17:28:25Z (+ offsets). All within ~24s of each other, ~9 minutes before this message. So no usable modification history, and it suggests the mount was provisioned/synced just now, not that files were edited.\n\nWhat I have NOT verified: I haven''t opened every leaf — Team/website/, Team/Offsite 2026/, Clients/Kaltwasser GmbH/, Clients/Saffron & Sage/, Clients/Aozora Ceramics/Rebrand/, and the rest of edge/. So \"identical\" is verified for: root, Team/ (6 entries), Finance/2025/ (3 files), Clients/ (3 folders), Clients/Aozora Ceramics/ (2 entries) — across all drives. I should state that scope honestly and offer to finish the walk.\n\nAlso worth flagging: six mirrors of the same data is a duplication risk — if these are the same store mounted six ways, fine; if they''re six independent copies, edits won''t propagate and you''ll get divergence. E.g., edit Budget 2025.xlsx on the FTP drive and the S3 copies keep the old numbers. That''s the practical implication.\n\nAlso drive 3''s scoping: it only sees Clients/, so anything you put in Team/ or Finance/ won''t be visible there.\n\nLet me write the answer with a comparison table.\n\nTable: rows = drives, columns = root items, Team items, Finance/2025, Clients, edge/, timestamps.\n\nLet me be precise about counts:\n- D1 SFTP 36600459: root 4 (Clients, Finance, Team, welcome.txt); Team 6; Finance/2025 3; Clients 3; Aozora 2; no edge.\n- D2 FTP d4dacf11: same, no edge.\n- D3 FTP 766b212a (scoped): root 1 (Clients); Clients 3; Aozora 2; nothing else visible.\n- D4 FTP 42ffd478: same as D1 pattern, no edge.\n- D5 WebDAV cc3fbded: same, no edge.\n- D6 S3 b4199497: root 5 (+edge); Team 6; Finance/2025 3; Clients 3; Aozora 2; edge 15.\n- D7 S3 1ae0b7b1: same as D6.\n\nTotal file inventory per full mirror: welcome.txt (1) + Team files (4) + Team folders (2) + Finance/2025 files (3) + Clients subfolders (3) + Aozora (1 file + 1 folder) + Kaltwasser + Saffron & Sage (unopened) + edge (15 at top).\n\nI''ll present a clean answer."}],"createdAt":1794332340706},{"type":"text","text":"**No — six of them are indistinguishable copies of one another, but one is a subset and two carry extra material.** I walked three levels deep (root → Team/ → Finance/2025/ and Clients/Aozora Ceramics/) on all seven.\n\n| Drive | Root | Team/ | Finance/2025/ | Clients/ | Aozora/ | `edge/` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** SFTP `36600459…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **2** FTP `d4dacf11…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **3** FTP `766b212a…` *(scoped)* | **1 item** | — | — | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **4** FTP `42ffd478…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **5** WebDAV `cc3fbded…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **6** S3 `b4199497…` | **5 items** | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | **15** |\n| **7** S3 `1ae0b7b1…` | **5 items** | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | **15** |\n\n**Where they agree** — drives 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7 hold the same tree, and not just the same names but the same byte sizes on every file I opened:\n\n- `welcome.txt` (182 B)\n- `Team/` → `website/`, `Offsite 2026/`, `新しいロゴ案.png` (11,491), `voice-memo.wav` (88,244), `brand-tokens.json` (240), `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` (574)\n- `Finance/2025/` → `Budget 2025.xlsx` (1,819), `expenses.tsv` (357), `invoices.csv` (440)\n- `Clients/` → `Aozora Ceramics/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/`; inside Aozora, `Proposal - signed.pdf` (789) plus `Rebrand/`\n\n**Where they differ**\n\n1. **Drive 3 (`766b212a…`) only ever sees `Clients/`.** No `Team`, no `Finance`, no `welcome.txt` — because it''s the folder-scoped one from the earlier listing. What it does expose matches exactly (Aozora''s `Proposal - signed.pdf` is 789 B, same as everywhere). So it isn''t a different store; it''s a keyhole into the same one. Anything you file under `Team/` or `Finance/` is invisible there.\n2. **Both S3 buckets carry an extra `edge/` folder**, 15 identical entries in each: `big/`, `bulk-2500/`, `deep/`, `docs/`, `images/`, `mislabeled/`, `preview/`, `special/`, `unicode/`, `empty-folder/`, `folder-marker/`, plus `empty.txt` (0 B), `one-byte` (1 B) and `no-content-type.bin` (4,096 B). That''s test scaffolding, not your work — the same fixture family as the 278 unread messages from `edge.test`.\n\n**Two caveats before you rely on this**\n\n- **\"Same files\" here means same names and same sizes, verified three levels deep.** I have not opened every leaf — `Team/website/`, `Team/Offsite 2026/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/`, `Rebrand/` and the rest of `edge/` are still unopened, so I can''t rule out a file that differs in content while matching in size.\n- **None of the timestamps are real.** The FTP drives report `modifiedAt: null` throughout; SFTP and WebDAV stamp every single entry `2026-11-10T17:28:49Z`; both S3 buckets stamp theirs `2026-11-10T17:28:25Z` with a few milliseconds'' spread. Those are mount times from about nine minutes before you asked, not edit history. MIME types differ cosmetically too — SFTP reports `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` as `text/markdown` and `expenses.tsv` as `text/tab-separated-values`, WebDAV flattens both to `text/plain`, and FTP and S3 report none at all.\n\nThe practical risk: if those six are genuinely **six independent copies** rather than one store mounted six ways, editing `Budget 2025.xlsx` on the FTP drive leaves the S3 and WebDAV copies on the old numbers, and you''ll have no signal telling you which is current — the timestamps are all identical. If they''re one store seen six times, this is harmless. That distinction is worth settling before you edit anything.\n\nWant me to finish the walk on the remaining folders, or read `welcome.txt` — it''s 182 bytes on every drive and may say outright which backend these point at?","createdAt":1794332340706}],"metadata":{"modelId":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash:floor","provider":"openai-compatible.chat"},"content":"**No — six of them are indistinguishable copies of one another, but one is a subset and two carry extra material.** I walked three levels deep (root → Team/ → Finance/2025/ and Clients/Aozora Ceramics/) on all seven.\n\n| Drive | Root | Team/ | Finance/2025/ | Clients/ | Aozora/ | `edge/` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1** SFTP `36600459…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **2** FTP `d4dacf11…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **3** FTP `766b212a…` *(scoped)* | **1 item** | — | — | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **4** FTP `42ffd478…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **5** WebDAV `cc3fbded…` | 4 items | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | — |\n| **6** S3 `b4199497…` | **5 items** | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | **15** |\n| **7** S3 `1ae0b7b1…` | **5 items** | 6 | 3 files | 3 folders | 2 | **15** |\n\n**Where they agree** — drives 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7 hold the same tree, and not just the same names but the same byte sizes on every file I opened:\n\n- `welcome.txt` (182 B)\n- `Team/` → `website/`, `Offsite 2026/`, `新しいロゴ案.png` (11,491), `voice-memo.wav` (88,244), `brand-tokens.json` (240), `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` (574)\n- `Finance/2025/` → `Budget 2025.xlsx` (1,819), `expenses.tsv` (357), `invoices.csv` (440)\n- `Clients/` → `Aozora Ceramics/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/`; inside Aozora, `Proposal - signed.pdf` (789) plus `Rebrand/`\n\n**Where they differ**\n\n1. **Drive 3 (`766b212a…`) only ever sees `Clients/`.** No `Team`, no `Finance`, no `welcome.txt` — because it''s the folder-scoped one from the earlier listing. What it does expose matches exactly (Aozora''s `Proposal - signed.pdf` is 789 B, same as everywhere). So it isn''t a different store; it''s a keyhole into the same one. Anything you file under `Team/` or `Finance/` is invisible there.\n2. **Both S3 buckets carry an extra `edge/` folder**, 15 identical entries in each: `big/`, `bulk-2500/`, `deep/`, `docs/`, `images/`, `mislabeled/`, `preview/`, `special/`, `unicode/`, `empty-folder/`, `folder-marker/`, plus `empty.txt` (0 B), `one-byte` (1 B) and `no-content-type.bin` (4,096 B). That''s test scaffolding, not your work — the same fixture family as the 278 unread messages from `edge.test`.\n\n**Two caveats before you rely on this**\n\n- **\"Same files\" here means same names and same sizes, verified three levels deep.** I have not opened every leaf — `Team/website/`, `Team/Offsite 2026/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/`, `Rebrand/` and the rest of `edge/` are still unopened, so I can''t rule out a file that differs in content while matching in size.\n- **None of the timestamps are real.** The FTP drives report `modifiedAt: null` throughout; SFTP and WebDAV stamp every single entry `2026-11-10T17:28:49Z`; both S3 buckets stamp theirs `2026-11-10T17:28:25Z` with a few milliseconds'' spread. Those are mount times from about nine minutes before you asked, not edit history. MIME types differ cosmetically too — SFTP reports `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` as `text/markdown` and `expenses.tsv` as `text/tab-separated-values`, WebDAV flattens both to `text/plain`, and FTP and S3 report none at all.\n\nThe practical risk: if those six are genuinely **six independent copies** rather than one store mounted six ways, editing `Budget 2025.xlsx` on the FTP drive leaves the S3 and WebDAV copies on the old numbers, and you''ll have no signal telling you which is current — the timestamps are all identical. If they''re one store seen six times, this is harmless. That distinction is worth settling before you edit anything.\n\nWant me to finish the walk on the remaining folders, or read `welcome.txt` — it''s 182 bytes on every drive and may say outright which backend these point at?"}','assistant','v2','2026-11-10T17:37:46.153Z','f0c4be98-b2fd-59d7-b11e-c921e7d22615');
