s, which could hold action items. Those are cheap. Let me also check snoozed and read-later.\n\nLet me batch: listEvents (this week), listEvents (next week), listEmails(drafts), listSnoozed, listReadLater.\n\nThat''s 5 calls. Good.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"Now list events for this week: 2026-11-09T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-16T00:00:00Z (Mon–Sun). Also maybe a wider window to catch the booking on 9 Nov. Let me list this week plus a bit beyond: 2026-11-09 to 2026-11-16. Also, I should check the \"Northbeam booking Lisbon→Tokyo Nov 9\" — a flight. Let''s list the week and also a slightly wider window (Nov 8–Nov 20) to catch travel/overflow.\n\nLet me call listEvents for 2026-11-09T00:00:00Z → 2026-11-16T00:00:00Z, and another for 2026-10-01 → 2026-11-09 to see what''s been happening / overdue? Hmm, better: one call for this week, one for the following week (16–23 Nov) to know what''s coming. And maybe one for the past ~3 weeks to spot things that were missed (since inbox stops 26 Sep).\n\nActually for a prioritized to-do list, overdue items matter. But calendar in the past doesn''t need action necessarily. The key action-generating thing: the inbox stopped 26 Sep 2026, six weeks ago. That''s itself the headline finding — no new mail for six weeks. Which means \"this week''s\" to-dos can''t be derived from mail; they have to come from the calendar and the state of the workspace.\n\nLet me do:\n1. listEvents 2026-11-09 → 2026-11-16 (this week)\n2. listEvents 2026-11-16 → 2026-11-23 (next week, for prep)\n3. Maybe listEvents for a wider window around: 2026-11-08 to 2026-11-15 already covered.\n\nAlso check drafts folder (pending unsent mail) — listEmails drafts. And read-later / snoozed lists, which could hold action items. Those are cheap. Let me also check snoozed and read-later.\n\nLet me batch: listEvents (this week), listEvents (next week), listEmails(drafts), listSnoozed, listReadLater.\n\nThat''s 5 calls. Good."}],"createdAt":1794332392691},{"type":"tool-invoc